questions.answer:
function serialize(form) {
  const out = {};

  for (const [key, value] of new FormData(form)) {
    if (key in out) {
      out[key] = [].concat(out[key], value);
    } else {
      out[key] = value;
    }
  }

  return out;
}

// <input name="name" value="Kakade">
// <input name="hobby" value="chess">
// <input name="hobby" value="code">
// → { name: 'Kakade', hobby: [ 'chess', 'code' ] }

FormData already applies the successful-control rules, so disabled and unchecked fields never enter the loop. The key in out check turns the second occurrence into an array; [].concat handles both the string-to-array and array-to-array cases. Values remain strings, so convert known numeric or boolean fields afterwards. File values would be File objects, which are not JSON-serializable — handle uploads separately or send FormData directly.

